# Audit results and detailed explanations, part D (q181-q240). Format: see explain_a.py.
E = {}
def e(qid, stem, exp, opts=None, q=None):
    E[qid] = dict(stem=stem, exp=exp, opts=opts, q=q)

e("q181", "The management function that monitors work", "The four management functions are planning, organizing, directing (leading) and controlling. Controlling means setting standards, measuring actual performance, comparing it with the plan and correcting problems. A budget-versus-actual report is a classic controlling tool.")
e("q182", "A teen buys a brand of sneakers after seeing", "Social influences come from other people: family, friends, reference groups and public figures such as influencers. Buying sneakers because popular influencers wear them is about fitting in with a group. Psychological influences are internal, like motives and attitudes.")
e("q183", "In a contract, what each party gives or receives", "Consideration is the value each side gives or promises in exchange, such as money for a car or work for a paycheck. Without it, a promise is usually just a gift and isn't legally enforceable. Capacity is legal ability to contract, and a tort is a civil wrong.")
e("q184", "A form of business organization whose primary goal", "A nonprofit exists to serve a mission, such as education, health or charity, rather than to make money for owners. It can earn more than it spends, but any surplus goes back into the mission instead of being paid to owners. Qualifying nonprofits can be tax-exempt.")
e("q185", "An agent who acts on behalf of a principal", "In an agency relationship the agent, such as an employee, broker or real estate agent, acts for the principal and owes fiduciary duties: loyalty, obedience to lawful instructions, reasonable care and accounting for money. Acting in their own interest at the principal's expense breaks those duties.")
e("q186", "Accountants must present financial information", "Objectivity means reporting facts accurately and without bias, even when the numbers are bad news for management. Users like investors and lenders rely on that neutrality. Shading numbers toward management or hiding information from auditors violates professional ethics.")
e("q187", "A company selling securities to the public must", "The Securities Act of 1933 requires companies offering securities to the public to disclose material information, including the risks, usually in a prospectus. Disclosure lets investors make informed decisions. Companies can't guarantee profits, and most securities carry some risk.")
e("q188", "Recognizing that you are getting frustrated", "Self-regulation is managing your own emotions and impulses once you notice them, such as pausing and calming down before responding. It builds on self-awareness. Social awareness is reading other people's emotions, a different emotional intelligence skill.")
e("q189", "Which statement best describes emotional intelligence", "Emotional intelligence is the ability to recognize and manage your own emotions and to recognize and respond well to others' emotions. It includes self-awareness, self-regulation, motivation, empathy and social skills, and unlike a fixed trait it can be improved with practice.")
e("q190", "A client tells a financial advisor she is scared", "Empathy means recognizing and acknowledging another person's feelings before moving on. 'That sounds really stressful' shows the advisor understands, and the follow-up offers support. The other responses dismiss, avoid or blame the client, which damages trust.")
e("q191", "A bank teller sees that a well-known local businessman", "Customer financial information is confidential. Banks have legal privacy obligations, such as under the Gramm-Leach-Bliley Act, and an ethical duty to protect it. The teller should use it only to complete the transaction and share it with no one who doesn't need it for work.")
e("q192", "Mateo realizes he gave a client the wrong interest", "Integrity means being honest and doing the right thing even when it's uncomfortable. Contacting the client right away, admitting the mistake and giving the correct rate protects the client's trust and the bank. Hiding it or blaming someone else makes the problem worse.")
e("q193", "An analyst notices that a monthly report takes", "Initiative is seeing something that could be better and acting on it without waiting to be told. Building a template that cuts four hours to 30 minutes shows initiative and creates real value. Assertiveness is about expressing your views, a different skill.")
e("q194", "A customer yells at a loan officer because", "Self-control is managing your own reactions under pressure. Staying calm instead of raising her voice keeps the situation from escalating and keeps the focus on solving the customer's problem. Yelling back would be aggressive; giving in to unreasonable demands would be passive.")
e("q195", "Which is the best way to use feedback", "Feedback helps only if you take it in and act on it. Listening without getting defensive, asking questions to understand it and making a specific improvement plan turns feedback into growth. Arguing with it or seeking only agreeable opinions wastes it.")
e("q196", "A bank switches to a new loan software system", "Adjusting to change means accepting it, learning what's needed and looking for benefits. Dana attends training and asks how the system can help clients. Refusing to switch, complaining or trying to escape the change are all resistance.")
e("q197", "Two coworkers disagree over who should present", "Collaborating seeks a win-win solution: both sides explain their needs, and they find a plan that satisfies both. It takes more time but builds commitment. Avoiding ignores the conflict, forcing imposes one side's view, and accommodating gives in to the other side.")
e("q198", "In a negotiation, the point at which you will walk", "Your bottom line, or resistance point, is the least favorable deal you'll accept; beyond it, walking away is better than agreeing. Setting it before negotiating protects you from accepting a bad deal under pressure. Your opening offer is where you start, not your limit.")
e("q199", "A team discusses options until every member", "Consensus building means discussing until every member can support the decision, even if it wasn't their first choice. Everyone's concerns are heard. Majority voting can leave a large minority unhappy, and groupthink is going along without voicing real concerns.")
e("q200", "A department manager gets employees to work", "Leadership is the ability to influence and inspire people toward a goal. Authority comes from a job title, but leaders earn followers through vision, example and trust. The manager motivates people by inspiring them, not just by her position.")
e("q201", "Which action best shows ethical leadership", "Ethical leaders model the behavior they expect, so following the same rules as employees builds credibility and a culture of integrity. Impossible goals push employees to cut corners (as at Wells Fargo), rewarding results 'no matter how' encourages misconduct, and hiding bad news breaks trust.")
e("q202", "A loan officer assumes an older applicant", "A stereotype is an assumption about a person based on a group they belong to, here age, rather than on the individual. Assuming an older applicant can't use an app and not mentioning it is a personal bias that gives that customer worse service.")
e("q203", "A financial advisor learns that a client's culture", "Cultural sensitivity is being aware of and respecting cultural differences and adapting how you work, without stereotyping. Including family in the meeting at the client's request respects how this client makes decisions, which builds trust and leads to a plan the family supports.")
e("q204", "Which is a healthy way to manage stress", "Healthy stress management includes planning and prioritizing, breaking big tasks into smaller steps and taking short breaks to recharge. Skipping sleep increases mistakes and stress, bottling up frustration builds tension, and piling on projects adds overload.")
e("q205", "An employee promises a client a report by Friday", "Managing commitments means meeting deadlines or, if you can't, telling people as early as possible and offering a realistic new date. Warning the client on Wednesday lets them plan and preserves trust. Missing the deadline silently or making excuses damages the relationship.")
e("q206", "Which behavior best shows treating others", "Treating others with dignity and respect includes listening fully before responding, valuing their input and handling problems privately. Correcting someone in front of a client, interrupting and using a nickname they dislike are all disrespectful.")
e("q207", "A manager who explains why a change is needed", "Leading change effectively means explaining why the change is needed, involving people in planning it and reinforcing progress by celebrating early wins. People resist change they don't understand or feel forced into, so communication and involvement increase buy-in.")
e("q208", "The shared values, beliefs and behaviors", "Organizational culture is the shared values, beliefs and norms, 'the way we do things here,' that shape how people behave and work together. A mission statement is a written statement of purpose, the chain of command is the reporting structure, and bylaws are governance rules.")
e("q209", "A team leader assigns the detail-oriented", "Leveraging personality types means matching tasks to people's natural strengths: the analytical person checks numbers and the outgoing person presents. That improves results and job satisfaction. It's based on observed strengths, not favoritism or stereotyping.")
e("q210", "Which is the most effective way to recognize", "Recognition motivates most when it's specific (naming exactly what the person did well), sincere and timely (soon after the action). Waiting months, praising vaguely or only rewarding people who ask makes recognition feel generic or unfair.")
e("q211", "What is the first step in the decision-making", "Decision-making starts with clearly defining the problem or decision, because a vague or wrong problem leads to the wrong solution. The usual steps are: define the problem, gather information, list alternatives, evaluate them, choose, act and review the results.")
e("q212", "A bank's online loan applications drop 30%", "Effective problem solving finds the root cause before jumping to solutions. Reviewing website data and talking to customers is gathering information to understand why applications dropped. Only then should the team brainstorm, choose and implement a fix.")
e("q213", "Which is the best example of time management", "Good time management means prioritizing: ranking tasks by importance and deadline and doing the most important work first. Constantly checking email interrupts focus, doing easy tasks first leaves important work undone, and saying yes to everything overloads you.")
e("q214", "An accountant follows the same checklist", "Systematic behavior means working in an organized, step-by-step way so nothing is missed. A month-end closing checklist is a perfect example, and it reduces errors in financial records. Creativity is generating new ideas, and initiative is acting without being told.")
e("q215", "Why does personal appearance matter for a financial", "People form first impressions within seconds, and clients are deciding whether to trust you with their money. A professional appearance signals competence and seriousness, which helps build trust from the start. It isn't legally required and never replaces knowledge.")
e("q216", "Which law sets the federal minimum wage", "The Fair Labor Standards Act of 1938 sets the federal minimum wage (currently $7.25 an hour), overtime pay of 1.5 times regular pay over 40 hours for non-exempt workers, and child labor rules. Sarbanes-Oxley, Dodd-Frank and Gramm-Leach-Bliley are finance laws.")
e("q217", "During a job interview for a bank analyst position", "Interviewers ask about mistakes to see honesty, accountability and whether you learn. The best answer briefly describes a real mistake, how you fixed it and what you do differently now. Claiming you never err, blaming others or dodging the question are red flags.")
e("q218", "Which belongs on a resume for a high school", "A resume should show relevant achievements and experience: DECA results show business knowledge and competitiveness, and a part-time job with responsibilities shows work ethic. Never include a Social Security number or photo, list every class or state salary expectations.")
e("q219", "When should you send a thank-you or follow-up", "A thank-you message within 24 hours of the interview shows professionalism and appreciation, and keeps you top of mind while the employer decides. Waiting a month or only following up if hired misses the chance to make a final good impression.")
e("q220", "Which is the best example of networking", "Networking means building relationships with people who can share advice, insight and job leads. An informational interview with a CPA family friend is a classic networking step. Mass-applying without research or waiting to be contacted isn't networking.")
e("q221", "A student who wants to work in banking volunteers", "Volunteering, such as with a free tax-preparation program like VITA, gives real experience, skills and references before a first full-time job. It shows initiative and interest in the field. It isn't a certification or a job application.")
e("q222", "Why is ongoing education important for finance", "Finance changes constantly: new laws (like the 2025 tax law), new products and new technology. Many credentials, including the CFP, CPA and FINRA licenses, require continuing education so professionals stay current and give accurate advice. It doesn't guarantee promotions.")
e("q223", "A bank creates a feature that rounds up every", "Innovation means turning a new idea into a product, service or process that creates value. A round-up feature that automatically moves spare change into savings is a new way to help customers save. Consolidation is merging firms, and compliance is following rules.")
e("q224", "Which is a SMART goal", "SMART goals are Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound. 'Save $1,200 by June 30 by depositing $100 a month' has a clear amount, a deadline and a plan. 'Save more money' and 'become rich someday' are vague wishes with no measure or deadline.")
e("q225", "Which job involves reviewing loan applications", "A loan underwriter reviews applications, including credit, income, debt and collateral, and decides whether the borrower meets the lender's standards and on what terms. Actuaries use statistics to measure risk, compliance officers ensure rules are followed and auditors check records.")
e("q226", "Which professional uses statistics and math", "Actuaries use math, statistics and data to estimate the probability and cost of future events like deaths, accidents and illnesses. Insurers use their work to set premiums and reserves. Underwriters then apply those guidelines to individual applicants.")
e("q227", "Which credential is most closely associated", "The Certified Financial Planner (CFP) credential is the best-known designation for personal financial planning, and CFP professionals must act as fiduciaries. CPAs specialize in accounting and tax, CFAs in investment analysis and CIAs in internal auditing.")
e("q228", "A new financial advisor joins the local chamber", "Chambers of commerce and community groups put advisors in regular contact with local business owners and professionals. Building those relationships leads to trust, referrals and new clients over time, which is the main purpose of joining.")
e("q229", "A company with strong corporate governance", "Investors and lenders see well-governed companies, those with independent boards, transparent reporting and strong controls, as lower risk. Lower perceived risk makes it easier to attract investors and borrow at lower interest rates. Governance doesn't exempt a company from regulation or taxes.")
e("q230", "Which committee of the board of directors oversees", "The audit committee, made up entirely of independent directors at listed companies, oversees financial reporting, internal controls and the relationship with the external auditors. Sarbanes-Oxley strengthened its role. The compensation committee sets executive pay.")
e("q231", "Having the CEO and the board chair be two different", "The board's job is to oversee the CEO. If the CEO also chairs the board, they're essentially supervising themselves. Separating the roles, or naming a lead independent director, gives the board independence to challenge management and hold it accountable.")
e("q232", "An employee who reports a suspected accounting", "Governance objectives include ethical behavior and accurate financial reporting, and every employee plays a part. Reporting a suspected violation through the ethics hotline uses the channel the company created for exactly this. Most fraud is discovered through tips.")
e("q233", "After a company adopts a policy requiring board", "Approval thresholds are a governance process: managers still run the business, but the biggest decisions need board review. That adds some time but gives independent oversight of high-risk choices, reducing the chance of costly mistakes.")
e("q234", "A loan officer nods, maintains eye contact", "Active listening means fully engaging with the speaker: paying attention, showing interest through eye contact and nodding, and paraphrasing to confirm you understood. Repeating the client's concern in her own words proves she heard it correctly and makes the client feel heard.")
e("q235", "A client says \"I'm fine with that plan\"", "When body language, such as crossed arms and a frown, contradicts words, the nonverbal signal often reveals true feelings. Asking an open question like 'What concerns do you have?' invites the client to share hesitations so the advisor can address them before moving forward.")
e("q236", "An advisor explaining investments to a 19-year-old", "Good communicators adapt to their audience. A first-time investor needs plain language and everyday examples, like comparing diversification to not putting all your eggs in one basket. Jargon or advanced strategies would confuse and discourage them.")
e("q237", "When a judge challenges your recommendation", "Defending ideas objectively means staying calm, explaining the facts and reasoning behind your recommendation and acknowledging any valid points the judge raises. That shows confidence and critical thinking. Getting louder or dismissing the judge looks defensive, and instantly giving up looks unprepared.")
e("q238", "Which is the best way to open a business presentation", "A strong opening states the purpose and previews what you'll cover, so the audience knows why to listen and where you're going. In a roleplay that means a one-sentence recommendation and an agenda. Apologizing or reading slides weakens your credibility.")
e("q239", "An analyst organizes a report from the most important", "Order of importance puts the most significant information first, so busy readers get the key points immediately even if they stop reading early. Chronological order follows time, spatial order follows physical layout and alphabetical order follows letters.")
e("q240", "Which graphic aid is best for showing how a company's", "A line graph is best for trends over time: years go along the bottom, revenue up the side, and the line shows rises and falls clearly. Pie charts show parts of a whole at one moment, organizational charts show reporting structure and flowcharts show process steps.")
